## Findings

Six findings came out of the run. Record each one VERBATIM as its own Finding node with the stated confidence. Each finding WAS_GENERATED_BY the execution, APPEARS_IN exactly the one figure named, and is RELEVANT_TO the open question `{QUESTION_ID}`.

1. "The fitted lag-1 history coefficient b1 is negative in 9 of 12 cells (median b1 = -0.14)."
   confidence: 0.8; appears in `figures/fig01_lag1_counts_hist.png`

2. "The population-average count history kernel decays within 150 ms, so a single lag captures most of the history dependence at 20 ms binning."
   confidence: 0.7; appears in `figures/fig03_history_kernel.png`

3. "The bootstrap 95% confidence interval excludes zero for the population mean b1 (mean -0.12, CI [-0.19, -0.05], 2000 draws)."
   confidence: 0.85; appears in `figures/fig05_bootstrap_ci.png`

4. "Spike-count variance exceeds Poisson expectation by 1.6x on average (Fano factor 1.6, range 1.2 to 2.1)."
   confidence: 0.75; appears in `figures/fig06_fano_factor.png`

5. "Cells with higher baseline firing rate show weaker lag-1 suppression (Spearman rho = 0.58 between rate and b1)."
   confidence: 0.6; appears in `figures/fig08_rate_vs_coef.png`

6. "The model with a lag-1 term improves held-out log-likelihood over the rate-only model in all 12 cells (median gain 0.031 bits per bin)."
   confidence: 0.65; appears in `figures/fig10_summary_grid.png`

